      "read_view": "Our algorithm operates on the augmented distribution underlying the\nproximal sampler of Lee, Shen, and Tian [17]. For a\nscale [MATH S2.SS2.p1.m1: \\eta\\in(0,1/\\beta]] , consider the joint law\n\n\n\n\n\n [MATH S2.E6.m1: \\pi_{\\eta}(\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y)\\propto\\exp\\Bigl\\{-V(x)-\\frac{\\|x-y\\|^{2}}{2\\eta}\\Bigr\\}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,.] \n\n(2.6)\n\n\nThe original proximal sampler algorithm performs Gibbs sampling on [MATH S2.SS2.p1.m2: \\pi_{\\eta}] .\nNamely, under [MATH S2.SS2.p1.m3: \\pi_{\\eta}] one may generate the joint state as\n\n\n\n\n\n [MATH S2.E7.m1: X\\sim\\mu\\,,\\hskip 20.00003ptZ\\sim\\mathcal{N}(0,I)\\ \\text{independently}\\,,\\hskip 20.00003ptY=X+\\sqrt{\\eta}\\,Z\\,.] \n\n(2.7)\n\n\nIts [MATH S2.SS2.p1.m4: X] -marginal is [MATH S2.SS2.p1.m5: \\pi_{\\eta}^{X}=\\mu] . Write [MATH S2.SS2.p1.m6: \\pi_{\\eta}^{Y}] for\nthe [MATH S2.SS2.p1.m7: Y] -marginal, so that\n [MATH S2.SS2.p1.m8: \\pi_{\\eta}^{Y}(\\mathrm{d}y)\\propto e^{-U_{\\eta}(y)}\\,\\mathrm{d}y] ; by\n(2.7), [MATH S2.SS2.p1.m9: \\pi_{\\eta}^{Y}=\\mu*\\mathcal{N}(0,\\eta I)] \nis a Gaussian smoothing of the target.\nWe use superscripts for marginal and conditional laws; in particular,\n\n\n\n\n\n [MATH S2.E8.m1: \\pi_{\\eta}^{Y\\mid X=x}=\\mathcal{N}(x,\\eta I)\\,,\\hskip 20.00003pt\\pi_{\\eta}^{X\\mid Y=y}(\\mathrm{d}x)\\propto\\exp\\Bigl\\{-V(x)-\\frac{\\|x-y\\|^{2}}{2\\eta}\\Bigr\\}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,.] \n\n(2.8)\n\n\nGibbs sampling alternates exact draws from the two conditional laws in\n(2.8).\nThe scale [MATH S2.SS2.p1.m10: \\eta] controls the strength of the quadratic coupling\npotential.\nDecreasing [MATH S2.SS2.p1.m11: \\eta] makes [MATH S2.SS2.p1.m12: X] and [MATH S2.SS2.p1.m13: Y] more tightly coupled and the\nconditional law of [MATH S2.SS2.p1.m14: X] given [MATH S2.SS2.p1.m15: Y] more localized and better\nconditioned, which reduces the cost of conditional sampling. The\nresulting transitions are also more local, however, and generally require\nmore Gibbs sampling iterations. Thus [MATH S2.SS2.p1.m16: \\eta] balances the cost of each\niteration against the number of iterations.\nThe convergence and query\ncomplexity are studied further in\n[6, 9, 15]."
    },
